WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.023 --> 00:00:02.464
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:00:02.464 --> 00:00:03.464
Thank you.

00:00:06.677 --> 00:00:09.633
All is well, how are you?
Very good, it's very good.

00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:12.283
Such a thrill to have
you here on the program.

00:00:12.283 --> 00:00:16.410
And also, I didn't get a
chance to formally thank you.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.510
You helped me out, you did
a bit on the Emmys with me

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:22.198
where I locked you in
an airtight container

00:00:22.198 --> 00:00:24.110
for the duration of the show.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.623
I think we have a still from that, yeah.

00:00:26.623 --> 00:00:29.330
(Audience and Conan laughing)

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.020
You look legitimately afraid there,

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.770
but it's one of my favorite
things that I ever got to do

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:35.970
and it was because I got
to do it with you, so...

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.320
Well, it was, you sent me the idea

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.520
and I thought it was a great idea.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:45.160
And it was fun, plus it's television.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.560
You know, the Academy, which
I'm proud of being part of.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.750
Yeah, yeah, it was a really fun night.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.363
I get it on the street a lot.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:55.927
Usually when I go into a town people say,

00:00:55.927 --> 00:00:59.690
"Your sure look like him."
Right, right, right.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.835
But now I get, I voted for you, you know?

00:01:02.835 --> 00:01:04.456
(Conan and audience laughing)

00:01:04.456 --> 00:01:06.723
I wanted you to live.
I wanted you to live.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.160
So reality show culture
now, so much to talk about.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.668
First of all, you know, I was so familiar

00:01:13.668 --> 00:01:17.390
with the television shows
that you did over the years.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:21.040
Loved the shows and before
that with your comedy albums.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.770
I didn't know much about
what you were doing

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.330
because you were a stand-up comedian.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.740
What kind of jobs did you have?

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.170
Well, I was an accountant.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.410
I had a degree in accounting,

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.200
and I worked as an
accountant and just hated it.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.130
[Conan] You hated being an accountant?

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.793
Yeah, I found it very boring.

00:01:36.991 --> 00:01:38.360
It wasn't as thrilling and sexy

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.348
as you thought it was going to be.

00:01:39.348 --> 00:01:40.985
No.
(audience laughing)

00:01:40.985 --> 00:01:45.640
And so I left accounting.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.283
I said, I've got to find out,

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:49.760
I've gotta take two years out of my life.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.020
I was single no
responsibility and so I said,

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.650
I've gotta take two years out of my life

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.030
and find out if I can make it in comedy

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.750
otherwise I'll spend the
rest of my life saying,

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.810
I wonder what would
have happened, you know?

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.588
And at least I can say, well,
I tried it and it didn't work.

00:02:03.588 --> 00:02:08.588
So I left accounting and I
would take part time jobs

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.430
and try to break into,
comedy writer, you know,

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.920
a funny disc jockey, whatever.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.536
I never thought I was
going to be a stand-up.

00:02:15.536 --> 00:02:18.040
I mean, that was the
furthest thing from my mind.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.397
And I once worked, one of the jobs I had,

00:02:20.397 --> 00:02:22.820
I'd take these temporary jobs.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:23.950
And I once worked for

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:28.610
the Illinois State Unemployment
Compensation Board.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:31.360
I worked behind the counter
at the unemployment office.

00:02:32.249 --> 00:02:35.760
And we got, at that time,
this was in the '50s.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.127
We got $65 a week.
That was your salary?

00:02:39.127 --> 00:02:41.743
$65 a week.
And the claimants was 55.

00:02:42.771 --> 00:02:44.880
(Conan and audience laughing)

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.063
People coming in, yeah.

00:02:46.063 --> 00:02:48.560
And they only had to
come in one day a week.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.503
[Conan] Right, right, (laughs)

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.680
(audience laughing)

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.910
It took me a few days to
figure, hey, for 10 bucks,

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.150
you know, I can have
four days off, you know?

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.093
Right. (laughs)

00:03:01.341 --> 00:03:02.650
So I got fired and I wound up--

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.220
Yeah, and then you came back in.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.710
And I wound up, hi guys,
see you next week, you know?

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:09.767
Now, you've been married, you mentioned

00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:11.520
this was when you were in single days.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.740
You've been married 43 years.
43, yeah.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.343
Yeah, incredible.
That's good.

00:03:15.343 --> 00:03:17.523
(audience applauding)

00:03:17.523 --> 00:03:20.860
What's the secret?

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:21.880
What do you think's the secret

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.490
of having a relationship
that lasts that long?

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:28.763
Well, I have a wife who,
she keeps me grounded.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.880
She doesn't let me get too
full of myself, you know.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.770
And I'm sure your wife is the same way.

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.830
Oh God, yeah, yeah, yeah.
(audience laughing)

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:37.970
Yeah, she is, yeah.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.330
She talked to me, in
the book about we took

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:45.330
a recreational vehicle trip
from hell with the kids

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:48.960
because you want it.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.960
So in LA we have a, on Wednesdays
they pick up the trash.

00:03:54.683 --> 00:03:56.720
And we have to separate it

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.910
into recyclable and non-recyclable.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:03.250
And so like on a Tuesday
late in the afternoon

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:05.023
Ginny will say to me, you know,

00:04:05.023 --> 00:04:07.200
"You gotta take out the
recyclable, you know,

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.900
'cause they're picking
it up tomorrow morning."

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.260
And I'll say to her, "Do
you think Paul Newman,

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.210
do you think Joanne
Woodward asks Paul Newman

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:19.110
to take out the recyclables?"
Right.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.530
And she says, "Well, if
you were Paul Newman,

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:23.181
I wouldn't ask you to to take out."

00:04:23.181 --> 00:04:26.223
(audience and Conan laughing)

00:04:26.223 --> 00:04:29.390
(audience applauding)

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.740
Keeps you grounded,
that's very good, yeah.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.199
I grew up watching you.

00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:37.400
You were one of my favorite
guests on "The Tonight Show."

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.290
You'd go on "The Tonight
Show with Johnny Carson."

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.870
And always a thrill to
see you on that show.

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:45.960
What was it like being on
that program as a guest?

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.130
'Cause I know that you and
Johnny were close, so was it--

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:50.100
Yeah, we were friends.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:55.100
And John, when the
monologue didn't go well

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.230
John kind of, he kind of
was going down with the ship

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.887
and he was taking everybody on
the show with him, you know?

00:05:04.887 --> 00:05:06.070
[Conan] Right, right.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:08.890
The guests, whoever, if the
monologue didn't go well.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:13.890
So I would, we made a
trip with the Rickles

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.640
and I'd be pre-interviewed,
you know, and say,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.210
Bob has a funny story
about, he just got back

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:22.720
from a trip with the Rickles
and he has a funny story.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.960
So I'd come out and it's
right there on the sheet

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.570
for the question, first
question for Johnny to ask.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:34.263
And Johnny would get that
look in his eyes and he'd say,

00:05:35.517 --> 00:05:40.517
"You ever been to India?"
(Conan and audience laughing)

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.530
I can't imagine a meaner
thing to do to a comedian.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:48.610
You've got nothing prepared, yeah.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.040
I knew, I'd never been to India.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.037
Yeah, yeah.
(audience laughing)

00:05:51.037 --> 00:05:53.710
And didn't have a joke
about being in India.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:55.607
Right, right, right.

00:05:55.607 --> 00:05:57.400
But what he would do, and Johnny,

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.560
he would do it for all the guests is,

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.290
he would never let you look bad.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:03.580
Right.
You know he would make sure.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.910
I was a stand-up comic,
he was a stand-up comic

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:10.910
so he kind of trusted that
I would somehow handle it.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.290
But he would never let you look bad.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.850
You had such a comfortable relationship

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:15.745
you pulled a prank on him once.

00:06:15.745 --> 00:06:18.490
I've heard about this, on the air

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.020
while Johnny was interviewing a guest.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:21.300
Yeah, John used to do,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.410
it used to be an hour and a half show.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:28.410
And usually the last half hour
was an author of some kind.

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.983
And at the end of the
couch there was this clock.

00:06:34.657 --> 00:06:36.050
And Johnny would look at it

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.640
to see how much time left in the show.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:38.473
[Conan] How much more does he have to do

00:06:38.473 --> 00:06:39.880
before he can wrap it up, yeah.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.340
So I did my slug with
him and so I moved here

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:45.210
and then we had another
guest and then I moved down

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.870
and I'm seated next to Ed, and Ed's

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.120
right next to the clock, you know?

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.500
So he brings out this author

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.650
and he was a particularly boring author.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:54.809
[Conan] Yeah, yeah.

00:06:54.809 --> 00:06:58.450
So every time John looked away I told Ed,

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.661
I said, "Move it back five minutes."

00:07:00.661 --> 00:07:04.494
(Conan and audience laughing)

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.410
So Ed would move it back
five minutes and John,

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.570
he would talk to the author
and he'd look over at the clock

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:17.205
and he'd go...
(Conan and audience laughing)

00:07:17.205 --> 00:07:20.419
(audience applauding)

00:07:20.419 --> 00:07:21.997
I can only imagine.

00:07:21.997 --> 00:07:23.558
I can imagine what that would feel like.

00:07:23.558 --> 00:07:24.391
We have a lot more to talk about.

00:07:24.391 --> 00:07:25.310
We're gonna take a break.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.640
More with Bob Newhart when we return.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:32.237
We'll see you in a second.
(audience applauding)

